te changes in orientation, though accumulated error makes sensor fusion useful.\n\nDevice-motion APIs combine gyroscope and [[Accelerometer Input]] to expose attitude as Euler angles, a matrix, or a quaternion. This lets gameplay use orientation without reconstructing it from raw sensor readings alone."],[20013,"Gyroscopic rigidity is the tendency of a spinning rotor to preserve its axis orientation. It increases with rotor mass, effective radius, peripheral mass concentration, and rotational speed. When torque tries to redirect the axis, the gyro responds through precession at right angles to the applied influence, providing the physical basis for attitude and heading instruments."],[36422,"H\n0\n: Column means are equal\n1 2\n( )\nkl A A A\nm m m = = … =\nH\na\n: Column means are unequal"],[35405,"H\na\n: The modification extends tube life.\nRephrasing these in terms of a parameter, the population mean,\nH\n0\n: m ≤ 3100\nH\na\n: m > 3100\nWhere m is the expected lifetime of picture tubes incorporating the modification. Recall that\nthe expected life of tubes without the modification was 3,100 hours.\nThe engineer must decide whether to reject H\n0\nand accept H\na\n(conclude that the modification\nis effective) or to not reject H\n0\n(conclude that lifetimes of tubes incorporating the modifica-\ntion are no longer than those of the original design).\nHypothesis testing leads to four possible situations, as shown in Figure 15.1.\nCorrect Type I error\nType II error\nCorrect\nDecision\nState\nof\nreality\nDo not reject the\nnull hypothesis\nReject the null hypothesis\nNull\nhypothesis\ntrue\nNull\nhypothesis\nfalse\nFigure 15.1 Outcomes of a hypothesis test.\n184 n n n r esearch Methods for Infor M at I on s yste M s n c Hapter 15\ne xamp L e 15.2\nAll figures and tables in this chapter appear on the companion.\nFailing to reject H\n0\nwhen H\n0\nis true and rejecting H\n0\nwhen it is false are correct decisions. The\nother two possibilities represent errors. Rejecting the null hypothesis when it is, in fact, true is\na Type I error, while failing to reject a false null hypothesis is a Type II error. In Example 2, a Type\nI error is committed if the engineer concludes that the modification is effective in prolonging\ntube life when it is not, and a Type II error is committed if the engineer fails to conclude that\nthe modification is effective when, in fact, it is not.\nThe Type I error is generally more serious. In Example 2, it might result in spending time and\nmoney to incorporate an ineffective modification, so hypothesis tests are constructed to\ncontrol the probability of a Type I error. This probability is indicated by a. The probability\nof a Type II error, discussed later in this chapter and in Chapter 16, “Single Large Sample\nTests,” is b.\nIn solving Example 2, we construct a rule by which we can decide if m is greater than 3,100.\nWe have seen the utility of estimating m with the sample mean X, so we will be convinced to\nreject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative if X is big enough, but how big is big\nenough? Since we also want to control a = P(Type I error), the probability that H\n0\nis rejected\nwhen it is true, consider the sampling distribution of X if H\n0\nis true, as shown in Figure 15.2.\n3100\na\nX under H\n0\nX\n−\n−\n*\nFigure 15.2 Sampling distribution of X given that H\n0\nis true.\nIf H\n0\nis true, then m = 3100, and we know from the central limit theorem that the sam-\npling distribution of X is approximately normal with mean 3,100 and standard deviation\n450 100 / . Choose a value for a, say 5%, and cut off an area of size a in the upper tail of the\ngraph of this distribution. Consider the value X"],[70839,"h\nh h\nh\nh\nlim\n(3 ) 9\nlim\n9 6 9\nlim\n(6 )\nlim6\n6 (0)\n6.\nh h\nh\nh\n0\n2\n0\n2\n0\n0\n\nThe same method of canceling common factors across the numerator and denominator also\nworks with nonrational expressions, as we demonstrate in the following examples.\nExampl E 7